## Claim
Text: "New Zealand's Abortion Legislation Act (2020) does not make it a legal requirement that babies born alive after an abortion are given medical support."
Claim author: Right To Life UK
Claim date: October 14, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20201204210013/https://perma.cc/CU7H-NQ83?type=image

## Initial Q&A
### Does the text of New Zealand's Abortion Legislation Act 2020 contain any explicit provisions regarding the medical treatment of infants born alive after an attempted abortion?
Answer: Based on the provided search result, the text of New Zealand's Abortion Legislation Act 2020 does not contain explicit provisions regarding the medical treatment of infants born alive after an attempted abortion. During the legislative process, the New Zealand Parliament considered but rejected "an amendment by National MP Simon O'Connor requiring medical intervention of unintended live births after attempted termination (37–80)."

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abortion_Legislation_Act_2020

### During the drafting and parliamentary debates of the Abortion Legislation Act 2020 in New Zealand, were there any proposed amendments specifically addressing the medical care of infants born alive after an abortion, and what was the outcome of those proposed amendments?
Answer: Yes, during the parliamentary debates for the Abortion Legislation Act 2020, National MP Simon O'Connor proposed "an amendment... requiring medical intervention of unintended live births after attempted termination." However, this proposed amendment was rejected by Parliament by a vote of 37 to 80.

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abortion_Legislation_Act_2020

## Final Judgement
